-- Q3737: Privacy Requirements and Data Protection [medium/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): GDPR Article 28(3)(a) requires that the processor shall process personal data only on documented instructions from the controller. The data processing agreement must specify the subject matter, duration, nature, and purpose of processing, along with the controller''s obligations and rights.
Wrong (B): GDPR does not permit transferring all liability to the processor. Both controllers and processors bear their own liability under Articles 82-83.
Wrong (C): The processor must not independently determine retention periods. Retention is governed by the controller''s instructions and data minimization principles.
Wrong (D): The processor is prohibited from using personal data for its own purposes, including marketing. Processing must follow the controller''s documented instructions.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3737;

-- Q3739: Privacy Requirements and Data Protection [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Following the Schrems II ruling (CJEU Case C-311/18), organizations relying on Standard Contractual Clauses under GDPR Chapter V must conduct a transfer impact assessment (TIA) to evaluate whether the destination country''s legal framework provides essentially equivalent data protection. If local surveillance laws undermine the SCCs'' effectiveness, supplementary measures must be implemented.
Wrong (A): SCCs alone are insufficient after Schrems II. The Court of Justice explicitly held that exporters must assess the legal framework of the recipient country before transferring data.
Wrong (B): Ceasing all transfers is not required. SCCs remain a valid mechanism when combined with a TIA and any necessary supplementary measures.
Wrong (C): Individual consent is not a practical alternative for large-scale, ongoing data transfers and does not address the systemic legal framework concerns raised by Schrems II.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3739;

