-- Q3771: SOC Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per AU-C Section 402.12 and AT-C Section 320, when a SOC report does not cover the user entity''s full reporting period, the user auditor must evaluate whether controls changed after the report period ended. The auditor should perform additional procedures — such as inquiries, reviewing subsequent reports, or requesting a bridge letter — to address the July 1 through December 31 gap.
Wrong (A): The SOC 1 report is not unusable simply because it covers only part of the fiscal year. The auditor can rely on it for the covered period while performing additional procedures for the gap.
Wrong (B): Relying on the report without additional procedures is inappropriate. The auditor must obtain evidence that controls operated consistently during the uncovered period.
Wrong (C): Requesting a new SOC 1 report for the second half is one possible but not required step. The auditor has multiple options to address the gap.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3771;

-- Q3772: SOC Engagements [easy/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (C): Per SSAE 18 (AT-C Section 320) for SOC 1 and AT-C Section 205 for SOC 2, SOC 1 examines controls relevant to user entities'' ICFR using control objectives defined by the service organization, while SOC 2 evaluates controls against the AICPA Trust Services Criteria covering security, availability, processing integrity, confidentiality, and privacy.
Wrong (A): This reverses the criteria. SOC 2 uses Trust Services Criteria, while SOC 1 uses control objectives related to ICFR — not the other way around.
Wrong (B): Both SOC 1 and SOC 2 can be issued as Type I or Type II. Neither engagement type is restricted to only one report format.
Wrong (D): This reverses the distribution rules. Both SOC 1 and SOC 2 reports are restricted-use. SOC 3 reports are the general-use alternative.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3772;

-- Q3774: SOC Engagements [hard/L2]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per AT-C Section 320, when significant system changes occur during the examination period, the service auditor must evaluate controls in both the pre-migration and post-migration environments. The system description should clearly describe the changes, and testing must cover both periods to provide complete assurance.
Wrong (B): Ignoring changes is inappropriate even if the new system meets the same criteria. The auditor must test controls in both environments and document the transition in the system description.
Wrong (C): Limiting the examination period to only post-migration would leave a coverage gap for the earlier period, providing incomplete assurance for the full examination window.
Wrong (D): Issuing two separate reports is neither required nor standard practice. A single report covering the full period with dual-environment testing is the appropriate approach.$EXPL$
WHERE id = 3774;

-- Q3776: SOC Engagements [medium/L1]
-- Issues: #3
UPDATE questions SET
  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (B): Per AICPA SOC guidance and AT-C Section 205, a readiness assessment is the recommended first step for organizations new to SOC examinations. It identifies gaps in the control environment, enables remediation, and prepares documentation before committing to a formal engagement.
Wrong (A): Proceeding directly to a 12-month Type II engagement without preparation risks significant exceptions and a potentially qualified or adverse opinion, wasting resources.
Wrong (C): While ISO 27001 is a valid security certification, it does not replace a SOC report. Many enterprise clients specifically require SOC 2 reporting for vendor assurance.
Wrong (D): Organizations cannot self-assess and issue their own SOC reports. SOC engagements must be performed by an independent CPA firm under SSAE 18 attestation standards.$EXPL$
